Great question! Flexbox is perfect for this. Here's how to center buttons both horizontally and vertically within their parent container:

## Basic Centering Solution

```css
.button-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;  /* Centers horizontally */
  align-items: center;      /* Centers vertically */
  gap: 1rem;                /* Consistent spacing between buttons */
}
```

## Common Issues and Solutions

**If buttons are still unevenly spaced:**

1. **Check for conflicting styles:**
```css
.button-container button {
  margin: 0;  /* Reset any default margins */
  flex-shrink: 0;  /* Prevent buttons from shrinking */
}
```

2. **Equal width buttons:**
```css
.button-container button {
  flex: 1;  /* Makes all buttons equal width */
  min-width: fit-content;  /* Prevents buttons from getting too small */
}
```

3. **Fixed spacing with gap:**
```css
.button-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1.5rem;  /* Consistent space between buttons */
}
```

## Complete Example

```css
.button-container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 1rem;
  min-height: 100px;  /* Ensure container has height for vertical centering */
}

.button-container button {
  padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
  margin: 0;  /* Reset margins */
  border: none;
  border-radius: 4px;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

## Troubleshooting Tips

- **Inspect the elements** in browser dev tools to see if there are inherited margins or padding
- **Check the parent container** - make sure it has defined dimensions
- **Look for conflicting CSS** - other styles might be overriding your flex properties
- **Test with different content** - sometimes button text length affects spacing

